When a listener’s callback, or the event target, belongs to a Window
whose associated Document isn’t fully active, Ladybird skips the
listener, as DOM’s inner invoke requires; and it skips an event handler
whose callback belongs to such a Window, as HTML’s event handler
processing algorithm does. The other engines refuse such listeners too,
each by its own mechanism. Blink’s JSBasedEventListener::Invoke()
returns early when the listener’s ScriptState fails ContextIsValid().
WebKit’s JSEventListener::handleEvent() returns early unless the
target document’s LocalDOMWindow isCurrentlyDisplayedInFrame(). Gecko’s
CallSetup::GetActiveGlobalObjectForCall(), which every listener call
goes through, refuses a callback whose window has no active document
(HasActiveDocument()). And DOMEventTargetHelper::DisconnectFromOwner()
drops a non-node target’s listeners when the target’s window goes away.
